# Line-ending policy. Scoped deliberately: only files whose exact bytes are
# hashed or compared are pinned here. Ordinary source normalises normally.

# Raw spectra are written by the instrument with CRLF endings, and
# write_provenance records the SHA-256 of those exact bytes for every source
# file. If git normalised them to LF in the index, a clone on Linux or macOS
# would check out different bytes and every hash in provenance.json would stop
# matching the file it describes - the experimental record quietly failing its
# own integrity check, on a machine where nothing looks wrong. The duplicate-
# content check in collect_warnings hashes the same bytes.
#
# This covers the hand-written bands.json and baseline.json too. They are not
# hashed today, but they are part of the same irreplaceable tree and there is
# no reason to let anything under data/raw/ be rewritten in transit.
data/raw/** -text

# PNGs are binary and git's content sniffing already treats them as such. Said
# explicitly because the six reference overlays are compared pixel by pixel by
# tests/test_raw_plot_reference.py, and a reference that depends on sniffing is
# a reference with a silent failure mode.
*.png binary

# Not pinned, and deliberately so: tests/fixtures/inspect_irradiation_sara.txt.
# It is CRLF on disk and is compared against captured stdout, but both sides of
# that comparison pass through universal-newline translation - read_text() on
# the fixture and text=True on the subprocess - so neither side can observe a
# line-ending change. Verified, not assumed. It normalises like any other text
# file.
